(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-epidemiology-quiz"]={"title":"Epidemiology — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Guests at one banquet eat a contaminated dish, and all of their exposure falls within a single incubation period. Which type of epidemic is this?","choices":["Propagated","Mixed","Common source, point","Common source, continuous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a common source epidemic exposes a group to the same source; when exposure is brief, within one incubation period, it is a point source. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A single survey finds that people who report a certain exposure have more of a disease than people who do not, and nothing more is known. How is this relationship best classed?","choices":["An association","Causation","A biological gradient","A temporal sequence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: an identifiable relationship between an exposure and a disease is an association; it implies the exposure might cause the disease, but finding an association does not make it causal. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 43 and 46"},{"q":"Air-quality ratings collected by sensors are which source of epidemiologic data?","choices":["Nonhealth-related sources","The environment","Individual persons","Health care providers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the environment supplies data through samples and sensors for environmental changes, including air-quality ratings. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which type of surveillance uses staff members to contact health care providers regularly for data?","choices":["Sentinel surveillance","Active surveillance","Passive surveillance","Syndromic surveillance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: active surveillance uses staff to regularly contact providers; it is the most accurate but the most expensive, and often used during outbreaks or seasonal events. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 19 and 20"},{"q":"Which critical characteristic of surveillance allows individual persons with disease to be identified for treatment or quarantine?","choices":["Timeliness","Representation","Sensitivity","Specificity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: sensitivity allows identification of individual persons with disease, to facilitate treatment, quarantine or other control measures. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Three studies each find that lung cancer mortality rises steadily from fewer than 10 to more than 40 cigarettes a day. Which of Hill's criteria does this rising trend most directly support?","choices":["Specificity","Temporal sequence","Plausibility","Biological gradient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a trend in risk with increasing dose, here more cigarettes per day, is a dose-response relationship, the biological gradient. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 49 and image on Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the first step in investigating a communicable disease outbreak?","choices":["Verify the diagnosis","Develop a focused hypothesis","Implement control measures","Establish its existence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the investigation begins by establishing the existence of an outbreak, then preparing for field work and verifying the diagnosis. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A survey asks every adult in a town whether they currently have asthma, and those answering yes are divided by the town's adults. What is it measuring?","choices":["Attack rate","Prevalence","Incidence","Crude mortality"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: all cases present at or during a time period, over the population, is prevalence; a survey of who currently has a condition measures it. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 27 and 28"},{"q":"Where does case surveillance begin?","choices":["Hospital billing offices","Community health surveys","Local and state health departments","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: case surveillance begins with local and state public health departments collecting data about cases; notifiable conditions are then reported on to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In the pyramid of associations, which explanation sits at the base?","choices":["Chance","Confounded","Non-causal","Causal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: from base to apex the pyramid runs chance, spurious or artifact, confounded, non-causal, and causal at the top. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"In the host-agent-environment triad, which of these is a host factor?","choices":["Crowded housing","A biting vector","A dietary allergen","Immunologic status"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: host factors are genetic endowment, immunologic status, personal characteristics, personal behavior, and definitive versus intermediate host in vector-borne disease. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 38, 39 and 40"},{"q":"What does collecting reportable disease data allow authorities to do?","choices":["Monitor disease, warn of outbreaks","Prove what causes each disease","Bill patients for public testing","Replace physicians' diagnoses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: data collection lets authorities monitor the disease and provides early warning of potential outbreaks. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How was the bacterium behind the 1976 Legionnaires' disease epidemic finally found?","choices":["A doctor isolated it on the first weekend","A survey of residents identified it","Genomic sequencing matched the cases","Testing of specimens thought dead"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: in December 1976, a laboratorian at the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention located the source bacteria by continuing to test specimens that were thought to be dead. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 64 and 68"},{"q":"Who is called the father of modern vital statistics?","choices":["John Snow","John Graunt","Hippocrates","William Farr"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: William Farr systematically collected and analyzed Britain's mortality statistics in the 1800s, developed life-table approaches and reported his findings to authorities and the public. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How are most diseases caused?","choices":["By microbes alone","By genes alone","By an interplay of factors","By one necessary factor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a cause is not generally a single factor; most diseases are caused by an interplay of different factors, a web of genes, microbes, environment, behavior and more. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 45 and image on Slide 45"},{"q":"What is a map marking where each person with a disease lived and worked called?","choices":["An epidemic curve","A surveillance indicator","An attack-rate table","A spot map"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: marking where persons with cholera lived and worked, as John Snow did, makes what is now called a spot map. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"For a chronic disease such as osteoarthritis, which has a long duration and an onset date that is hard to pinpoint, which measure is usually used?","choices":["Prevalence","Incidence","Case fatality rate","Attack rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: prevalence rather than incidence is often measured for chronic diseases such as diabetes or osteoarthritis, which have long duration and hard-to-pinpoint onset. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is genomic epidemiology?","choices":["Monitoring the genetic makeup of pathogens","Tracing inherited disease in families","Testing new vaccines in clinical trials","Mapping cases by home and workplace"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: genomic epidemiology is the process of monitoring the genetic makeup of the pathogens that cause disease, made possible by advances in sequencing. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 12 and 69"},{"q":"A prearranged group of providers agrees to report every case of a condition, tracking it for a much larger population. Which type of surveillance is this?","choices":["Sentinel surveillance","Passive surveillance","Active surveillance","Syndromic surveillance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: sentinel surveillance uses a prearranged group of reporting sources, usually providers, as a smaller representative group tracking on behalf of a larger population. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"An infection spreads from person to person through a community. Which type of epidemic is this?","choices":["Common source, continuous","Endemic","Propagated","Common source, point"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a propagated epidemic is person-to-person transmission, reflecting spreading infection that may be extended. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 36 and 55"},{"q":"Which three phenomena are assessed in analytic epidemiology?","choices":["Exposure, latency and outcome","Host, agent and environment","Person, place and time","Sensitivity, specificity and timeliness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the basic triad of analytic epidemiology is host, agent and environment. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Among the five W's of epidemiology, which one names the health event or diagnosis?","choices":["Who","Where","Why","What"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: What is the health event or diagnosis; Who is the person or population, Where the location, When the time period and Why the causes. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In 1994, what change in approach to youth smoking did the Surgeon General's report recommend?","choices":["Leave the issue to individual families","Concentrate efforts on adult smokers","Treat it as a community and population issue","Focus campaigns on individual young people"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the report recommended moving from campaigns focused on individual young people to addressing youth smoking as a community and population issue. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which measure counts only the new cases that arise during a given time period?","choices":["Crude mortality rate","Incidence","Prevalence","Case fatality rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the numerator of incidence is the new cases that occurred during the time period; incidence is likened to a speedometer. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Epidemiological studies can bring about major health policy changes when they meet which condition?","choices":["Paired with matching political support","Rigorously done, reported and communicated","Large enough to include every resident","Approved by the affected communities"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: epidemiological studies can result in major health policy changes if they are rigorously conducted, reported and communicated; the evidence they supply is epidemiology's main contribution to policy. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which branch of epidemiology examines how a disease is distributed in a population by time, place and person?","choices":["Genomic epidemiology","Experimental epidemiology","Descriptive epidemiology","Analytic epidemiology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: descriptive epidemiology examines a disease's distribution in terms of time, place and person, and uses it to formulate hypotheses. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 32 and 33"},{"q":"What is a pandemic?","choices":["A worldwide epidemic","A disease at low, steady levels","An isolated, irregular case","A disease reported by law"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: pandemics are diseases that are worldwide epidemics. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which use of epidemiological training develops a hypothesis for a health concern and designs an experimental model to test it?","choices":["Experimental epidemiology","Program evaluation","Disease prioritization","Public health surveillance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: experimental epidemiology develops a hypothesis for a health concern and designs an experimental model to test it. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 7 and 8"},{"q":"What is herd immunity?","choices":["Antibody made after a single infection","The share of a population that is immune","Resistance one person inherits from parents","A vaccine given to every member of a herd"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: herd immunity is the cumulative number of immune persons in a population, or the percentage of the population that is immune. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which 17th-century figure pioneered tabulating population health and mortality as rates, ratios and proportions?","choices":["Doll and Hill","John Graunt","William Farr","Hippocrates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: John Graunt pioneered tabulating population health and mortality in rates, ratios and proportions, noting male-female differences, high infant mortality, urban-rural differences and seasonal variation. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 10 and 11"}]};
